@page "/test/compression"
@model OperaLegacyLab.Web.Pages.Test.CompressionModel
@using OperaLegacyLab.Web.Infrastructure
@{
    var code = Model.Lab.Code;
}
<h1>HTTP compression test</h1>
<p>Two separate things, often lumped together as one "does it support
compression?" question: whether your browser <b>asks</b> for a compressed
response at all (the <tt>Accept-Encoding</tt> request header - the only
"compression" a browser itself ever sends; browsers this old have no way to
compress a request body), and whether it can actually <b>decompress</b> one
if the server sends it regardless.</p>

<p><tt>Accept-Encoding</tt> header this browser just sent: <tt>@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.AcceptEncoding) ? "(not sent)" : Model.AcceptEncoding)</tt><br>
<tt>TE</tt> header: <tt>@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.Te) ? "(not sent)" : Model.Te)</tt></p>

<p>The three links below each ignore whatever Accept-Encoding said above and
force a compressed response anyway, specifically to find out what the
browser can really handle rather than only what it claims to. Each should
show one readable sentence if decompression worked, or garbled text, a
download prompt, or nothing at all if it didn't:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="@Markup.U("/test/compression/gzip", code)">gzip</a> - the one
virtually every modern server and browser agrees on</li>
<li><a href="@Markup.U("/test/compression/deflate", code)">deflate
(zlib-wrapped)</a> - what HTTP's "deflate" Content-Encoding token is
actually supposed to mean per RFC 2616</li>
<li><a href="@Markup.U("/test/compression/deflate-raw", code)">deflate
(raw)</a> - what a lot of real servers sent instead, historically, under the
same "deflate" name - a well-known interop mixup from that era, included in
case the zlib-wrapped one above fails but this one doesn't</li>
</ul>

@Html.Raw(SelfReport.Form(code,
    "Which of the three above showed readable text - gzip, deflate (zlib), deflate (raw), some combination, or none? Note anything unusual (download prompt, blank page, garbled text)."))
